  "Name": "Ciosek2000-NCNBPIF-m7-LHL-HLH",
  "Source": "J. Ciosek, \"Narrow-band interference filters with unconventional spacer layers,\" Appl. Opt. 39, 135-140 (2000). doi:10.1364/AO.39.000135 - Fig. 7 curve 3, Fig. 8, Fig. 6 curve 1, Fig. 4 (p.138-139); Eq. (4) with m = n = 7",
  "Notes": "Narrow-band filter with an unconventional spacer, designed at 632.8 nm; its transmittance peak falls at 652.22 nm where the two mirrors' reflection phases sum to 360 deg\nSpecification: Single narrow transmittance peak at lambda' = 652.22 nm without a half-wave spacer.\nReported: T = 1 (R = 0) at 652.22 nm (text p.138, Fig. 2 vertical line, Figs. 6-8; Eq. 13). Fig. 8 (approximate): broader dip than the Fabry-Perot filters, R < 0.5 roughly from 651.5 to 653.0 nm. Fig. 4: transmittance change for a 0.01 relative error in one layer is largest (~15 a.u.) for layer 16 of 30.\nNotes: Paper notation: 632.8[(0.5L H 0.5L)^7(0.5H L 0.5H)^7] (30 layers after merging). H = quarter wave of n = 2.25, L = quarter wave of n = 1.45 at reference_nm, no dispersion; n_S = n_0 = 1 (Table 1 footnote, Sec. 3), so incident and substrate are both air. Adjacent layers of the same material in the paper's symbol string are merged into one layer (e.g. ...0.5L)(0.5L... becomes one L), which reproduces the paper's own layer counts (27 in Fig. 3, 30 in Fig. 4). Layers are listed in the order the paper writes the symbol string; the paper's Eq. (4) 'S | ... | P' suggests the string starts at the substrate, but because both outer media are n = 1 and the stack is lossless, R and T do not depend on which side faces the light (only the reflection phase does). Normal incidence.\nLayers from Fig. 7 curve 3, Fig. 8, Fig. 6 curve 1, Fig. 4 (p.138-139); Eq. (4) with m = n = 7; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T peak 100.000 % at 652.218 nm; paper T = 1 at 652.2 nm.",
